### What cost are you offloading?

JWT verification involves a cryptographic signature check using the issuer's public key. If the gateway fetches the key over the network per request, that network round trip dominates. Even with a cached key, the crypto itself costs CPU at high throughput.

```text
// Per-request JWT verification cost:
// Network fetch of public key: ~5ms (if not cached)
// RSA-2048 signature check: ~0.2ms
// ECDSA-P256 signature check: ~0.1ms

// At 50,000 req/s with uncached RSA:
// 50,000 * 5.2ms = 260,000ms = 260 CPU-seconds/second (impossible!)

// At 50,000 req/s with cached RSA:
// 50,000 * 0.2ms = 10,000ms = 10 CPU-seconds/second (manageable)

// Caching the key alone gives a 26x improvement
```

### How does local verification with cached JWKS work?

A signed JWT is self-contained. The gateway only needs the issuer's public keys, which rarely change. Fetch the JWKS once, cache it, and verify every token locally.

```java
package com.bank.gateway.security;

import org.springframework.security.oauth2.jwt.JwtDecoder;
import org.springframework.security.oauth2.jwt.NimbusJwtDecoder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class CachedJwtConfig {

    @Bean
    public JwtDecoder jwtDecoder() {
        // Fetches JWKS once, caches keys in memory
        // Automatically refreshes when an unknown keyId appears
        return NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(
                "https://auth.nbi.example.com/.well-known/jwks.json")
                .build();
    }
}
```

```text
// Startup: fetch JWKS from auth server → cache 3 public keys
// Request 1: verify JWT with cached key → 0.2ms (no network)
// Request 50,000: same cached key → 0.2ms
// Key rotation: new keyId appears → auto-refresh JWKS → cache new key
// Total network calls: ~1 per key rotation (every few hours)
```

### How does sidecar verification distribute the CPU load?

Putting verification in a sidecar proxy next to each service spreads the crypto work across many instances instead of concentrating it in the gateway.

```text
// Centralized (gateway only):
// 50,000 req/s → all JWT verification on 3 gateway replicas
// Each replica: ~17,000 verifications/s

// Distributed (sidecar per service):
// 50,000 req/s → verification in sidecars across 20 service instances
// Each sidecar: ~2,500 verifications/s
// Gateway freed from crypto work entirely

// Sidecar: Envoy, Istio, or a custom proxy
// Each sidecar has its own cached JWKS
```

### How does a dedicated auth tier issue a context token?

A front tier verifies the heavy JWT once, then mints a small internal context token that inner services trust cheaply.

```java
// Flow:
// 1. Client sends request with heavy JWT to Auth Tier
// 2. Auth Tier verifies JWT (signature, expiry, scopes)
// 3. Auth Tier issues a lightweight context token:
//    {userId: "CUST-1001", roles: ["TRANSFER"], iat: now, exp: now+60s}
//    Signed with a symmetric key (HMAC) → cheap to verify
// 4. Forward context token to API Gateway / services
// 5. Services verify context token: HMAC check = ~0.01ms (vs RSA 0.2ms)

// Heavy work (RSA verify): happens ONCE at auth tier
// Cheap work (HMAC verify): happens at every service, but costs almost nothing
// Especially useful when one request fans out to 5+ internal services
```

### How does introspection caching work for opaque tokens?

Opaque tokens (not self-contained JWTs) require a network call to the auth server's introspection endpoint. Cache the result per token.

```java
package com.bank.gateway.security;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.time.Duration;

@Component
public class IntrospectionCache {

    private static final Duration CACHE_TTL = Duration.ofSeconds(30);
    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final AuthServerClient authClient;

    public IntrospectionCache(StringRedisTemplate redis,
                               AuthServerClient authClient) {
        this.redis = redis;
        this.authClient = authClient;
    }

    public IntrospectionResult introspect(String token) {
        // Check cache first
        String cached = redis.opsForValue().get("introspect:" + hash(token));
        if (cached != null) {
            return deserialize(cached); // Cache hit: no network call
        }

        // Cache miss: call auth server
        IntrospectionResult result = authClient.introspect(token);
        redis.opsForValue().set(
                "introspect:" + hash(token),
                serialize(result),
                CACHE_TTL);
        return result;
    }

    private String hash(String token) {
        // Hash the token for the cache key, never store raw tokens
        return null; // SHA-256 hash
    }

    private String serialize(IntrospectionResult result) { return ""; }
    private IntrospectionResult deserialize(String json) { return null; }
}
```

```text
// Without cache: 50,000 req/s * 5ms introspection call = 250,000ms
// With 30s TTL cache: ~1 call per unique token per 30s
// If 1000 unique tokens active: ~33 calls/s instead of 50,000
// 99.93% reduction in auth-server calls
```

### Why is ECDSA verification cheaper than RSA?

Elliptic-curve cryptography achieves the same security with much smaller keys and signatures, giving better throughput.

```text
// Security equivalence:
// RSA-2048 ≈ ECDSA-P256 (both ~128-bit security)

// Key sizes:
// RSA-2048: public key = 256 bytes, signature = 256 bytes
// ECDSA-P256: public key = 64 bytes, signature = 64 bytes
// 4x smaller keys and signatures

// Verification throughput (approximate, platform-dependent):
// RSA-2048: ~15,000 verifications/second per core
// ECDSA-P256: ~20,000+ verifications/second per core
// Measure on your platform; ECDSA is typically faster or comparable

// JWT header: {"alg": "ES256"} instead of {"alg": "RS256"}
// Smaller tokens, faster verification, same security
```

### What are common mistakes? (wrong vs correct)

```java
// WRONG: network call to auth server per request
IntrospectionResult result = authClient.introspect(token); // 5ms per call
// At 50K req/s → auth server overwhelmed

// CORRECT: cache JWKS locally for self-contained JWTs
JwtDecoder decoder = NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(jwksUrl).build();
// Local verification: 0.2ms per call, no network

// WRONG: never refreshing cached keys
// Key rotation happens → new tokens fail verification!

// CORRECT: refresh when unknown keyId appears
// NimbusJwtDecoder handles this automatically
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer measures verification throughput on their platform, standardizes on ECDSA for new systems, and combines caching with a dedicated auth tier for high-fan-out scenarios.

```text
// Senior offloading strategy:
// 1. Cache JWKS locally (eliminates network cost)
// 2. ECDSA tokens (faster verification, smaller)
// 3. Sidecar verification (distribute CPU across services)
// 4. Dedicated auth tier + context token (for fan-out scenarios)
// 5. Introspection cache for opaque tokens (30s TTL)
// 6. Key rotation: auto-refresh on unknown keyId
// 7. Self-contained JWTs cannot be revoked → short TTL + revocation check
```
